# Regenerate the Verilog netlist Vivado reads, PRESERVING yosys cell names.
#
# WHY -norename
# -------------
# The first attempt at "nextpnr place -> Vivado route" applied 0 of 141548
# placement constraints. The cause was not cell packing (only 275 $PACKER and
# 751 $LUT$ split cells exist out of 70774). It was that the two files were
# written at different points in the flow with different NAME SETS:
#
#   netlist_for_vivado.v   cells named  _62672_
#   nextpnr placed JSON    cells named  $flatten\...\worker.\compare.$lt$...
#
# yosys write_verilog documents this:
#
#   -norename
#       without this option all internal object names (the ones with a dollar
#       instead of a backslash prefix) are changed to short names in the
#       format '_<number>_'.
#
# So the default rewrote every internal name and nothing could ever have
# matched. -norename keeps the names nextpnr also carries, making the placement
# reattachable by name.
#
# SOURCE
# ------
# _st, not _yb. The Y-band run (92.36 MHz) was reported as the best result for
# most of this session, but the striped run is better: 102.15 MHz, also 0
# unrouted. Both constrain the same 420 BRAMs over the same X/Y span and differ
# only in the allocation, so Y-band is a ~10% REGRESSION, not the +12% win it
# was recorded as.
#
# For this script the choice is cosmetic -- _st and _yb are byte-identical in
# size (61431662) because they are the same synthesis output differing only in
# BEL attributes, so the cell set and hence the emitted Verilog are the same.
# Using _st anyway so the provenance points at the run actually worth beating.
#
# Using the pre-pack synthesis JSON (not the placed one) because Vivado must
# link ordinary Xilinx primitives, not nextpnr's packed LC/SLICE cells.
#
# Run:  yosys -s make_vivado_netlist.ys

read_json out_nm1_nosr/am01_qmtech_top_v68.json
write_verilog -norename out_nm1_nosr/netlist_norename_v68.v
